## Bug

`ponytail-activate.js` reads `~/.claude/settings.json` to detect whether a statusline is configured. If the file contains valid JSON `null` (unlikely but valid), `JSON.parse()` returns `null` and the next line crashes:

```js
// hooks/ponytail-activate.js lines 45-48
const settings = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(settingsPath, 'utf8'));
if (settings.statusLine) {   // ← TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading 'statusLine')
  hasStatusline = true;
}
```

The crash IS caught by the outer `try/catch` at line 68, so the hook doesn't die — but the `hasStatusline` check never runs, so the statusline setup nudge is silently suppressed. The user sees no offer to configure the statusline badge.

## Reproduce

```sh
echo 'null' > ~/.claude/settings.json
# start a Claude Code session
# ponytail activates fine, but never offers to set up the statusline badge
```

## Live reproduction

```
Bug #4 CONFIRMED: threw inside try/catch:
  Cannot read properties of null (reading 'statusLine')
hasStatusline was NOT set, nudge suppressed: false
```

## Fix — one line guard

```js
// before
if (settings.statusLine) {
// after
if (settings && typeof settings === 'object' && settings.statusLine) {
```

This is the same null-guard pattern used elsewhere in Node config readers. No behaviour change for normal `settings.json` files.

## Bug

`hooks/ponytail-mode-tracker.js` has two independent code branches that both call `writeHookOutput()` (which calls `process.stdout.write()`). When a user prompt triggers both branches simultaneously, two JSON objects are concatenated on stdout: `{...}{...}`. This is invalid JSON and Claude Code's hook parser will fail to parse it.

## Trigger condition

Any prompt that contains:
1. `/ponytail off` **AND**  
2. `stop ponytail` or `normal mode`

Examples:
- `/ponytail off, normal mode`
- `/ponytail off — stop ponytail please`

## Root cause

```js
// Branch 1 (line 41-44): fires for /ponytail off
} else if (mode === 'off') {
  clearMode();
  writeHookOutput('UserPromptSubmit', 'off', 'PONYTAIL MODE OFF');  // WRITE #1
}
// ... end of if block

// Branch 2 (line 48-51): ALWAYS runs after Branch 1, independently
if (/\b(stop ponytail|normal mode)\b/i.test(prompt)) {
  clearMode();
  writeHookOutput('UserPromptSubmit', 'off', 'PONYTAIL MODE OFF');  // WRITE #2 — double!
}
```

Branch 2 is not guarded against Branch 1 having already handled the deactivation.

## Fix

```js
let handled = false;

if (/^[/@$]ponytail/.test(prompt)) {
  // ... existing command parsing ...
  if (mode && mode !== 'off') {
    setMode(mode);
    writeHookOutput('UserPromptSubmit', mode, 'PONYTAIL MODE CHANGED — level: ' + mode);
    handled = true;
  } else if (mode === 'off') {
    clearMode();
    writeHookOutput('UserPromptSubmit', 'off', 'PONYTAIL MODE OFF');
    handled = true;
  }
}

if (!handled && /\b(stop ponytail|normal mode)\b/i.test(prompt)) {
  clearMode();
  writeHookOutput('UserPromptSubmit', 'off', 'PONYTAIL MODE OFF');
}
```

A test case should be added to `tests/hooks.test.js` for a prompt like `'/ponytail off, normal mode'` asserting exactly one JSON object on stdout.

## Bug

`writeDefaultMode()` in `hooks/ponytail-config.js` (line 86) overwrites the entire config file with only `{"defaultMode": "...`}`. Any additional keys the user has in their `~/.config/ponytail/config.json` are silently lost.

```js
// current — destroys everything except defaultMode
fs.writeFileSync(configPath, JSON.stringify({ defaultMode: normalized }, null, 2), 'utf8');
```

## Steps to reproduce

```sh
# user config with extra key
echo '{"defaultMode": "full", "myCustomKey": "preserved"}' > ~/.config/ponytail/config.json

# run /ponytail default lite from Claude Code / pi
# → config is now {defaultMode: lite}
# myCustomKey is gone
cat ~/.config/ponytail/config.json
```

This also means any future schema additions to `config.json` (e.g. per-project overrides, UI theme, feature flags) will be wiped on the first `/ponytail default` call — a forward-compatibility hazard.

## Fix — read-modify-write

```js
function writeDefaultMode(mode) {
  const normalized = normalizeConfigMode(mode);
  if (!normalized) return null;

  const configPath = getConfigPath();
  fs.mkdirSync(path.dirname(configPath), { recursive: true });

  // Read existing config to preserve unknown keys
  let existing = {};
  try {
    const raw = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(configPath, 'utf8'));
    if (raw && typeof raw === 'object') existing = raw;
  } catch (e) {}

  fs.writeFileSync(configPath, JSON.stringify({ ...existing, defaultMode: normalized }, null, 2), 'utf8');
  return normalized;
}
```

It will be great of we add something like this to the Pi extension. I added to my env and love it :-)

